# sparx-app-env — AKS variant. Near-identical to k8s/local/app-env-configmap.env,
# because the public hostnames, the worker dispatch table and the media layout do
# not depend on where the cluster runs. It diverges in exactly three places, all
# of them "this one is production and that one is a laptop":
#
#   REDIS_URL             absent here (see below)
#   SPARX_EMAIL_PROVIDER  `mailgun` here, `console` locally — dev must not send
#   SPARX_MAILGUN_DOMAIN  only meaningful alongside the provider above
#
# Everything else must stay byte-identical; a value that belongs in only one of
# the two is a value that will be wrong in the other.
#
# DATABASE_URL is NOT here — it is a secret, and it points at the managed
# Flexible Server (psql-sparx-prod-cus, VNet-private). See README.md.
# dotenv format because kustomize's configMapGenerator reads it via `envs:`.
# Secrets still come from `sparx-app-secrets` (see secrets.example.env).
#
# Public hostnames are DELIBERATELY unchanged from production. Cloudflare
# Tunnel lets the same names resolve to this workstation, so every OAuth
# callback, webhook target (Stripe, Mailgun, the social platforms), Better Auth
# cookie scope and MCP resource identifier stays valid. Changing them would mean
# re-registering callbacks with six external providers for no benefit.

# --- Event broker ----------------------------------------------------------
# EVENT_BROKER names a CAPABILITY, not a cloud. It replaced the absence of
# GCP_PROJECT_ID as the thing that decided where events went — a switch that
# lived in nine domain packages and, when the platform moved off GCP, silently
# selected a development transport in production.
#
# What that transport was: a fire-and-forget HTTP hand-off (SPARX_DEV_WORKER_ROUTES,
# removed below). The publisher POSTed each event to a worker's endpoint and did
# not await it. No queue, no retry, no acknowledgement, no dead-letter — an event
# published while its worker was restarting was gone, and every publish still
# reported success. The block that used to sit here named this as "the one genuine
# gap in this migration"; this is that gap closed.
#
# NATS JetStream now runs in-cluster (k8s/self-hosted/nats.yaml) on every
# provider, which is why it was chosen over a managed queue: nothing here changes
# when the platform moves. Publishes are acknowledged by the server, consumers are
# durable and resume where they stopped, and a failed handler is redelivered.
#
# Unset is NOT a fallback. packages/events/src/transport.ts throws under
# NODE_ENV=production if this is missing, so a misconfiguration fails a rollout
# instead of quietly dropping events.
EVENT_BROKER=nats
EVENT_BROKER_URL=nats://nats.sparx-prod.svc.cluster.local:4222

# --- Public origins (unchanged from prod) ----------------------------------
BETTER_AUTH_URL=https://app.sparx.works
MCP_RESOURCE_URL=https://mcp.sparx.works/mcp
MCP_AUTH_SERVER_URL=https://app.sparx.works
SITE_MCP_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://mcp.sparx.zone
CUSTOMER_AUTH_URL=https://api.sparx.works
CUSTOMER_MCP_LOGIN_PAGE=/account/login
# The operator console's own origin. Better Auth validates request origins
# against this, so it must be the host apps/admin is SERVED from — not
# BETTER_AUTH_URL, which belongs to the tenant-facing auth instance on
# app.sparx.works. Missing here originally, which left it defaulting to
# http://localhost:3002 and rejecting every admin sign-in as "invalid origin".
OPERATOR_AUTH_URL=https://admin.wize.works
# The BROWSER-facing workbench origin, and distinct from
# SPARX_DASHBOARD_INTERNAL_URL below (a cluster DNS name a browser cannot
# resolve). api-rest builds the /accept-invite link in a team invitation from
# this; unset, it fell back to http://localhost:3001, so every invitation email
# production has ever sent carried a link that only works on the sender's own
# laptop. Missing from the GCP configmap too — this is a pre-existing bug, not a
# migration regression.
SPARX_DASHBOARD_URL=https://app.sparx.works
# The ONE name for the workbench's public origin — every link a service puts in an
# email resolves through it (@wizeworks/links/server appOrigin). Four variables used to
# mean this and each emitter read a different one with its own fallback, so which
# host an email pointed at depended on which service sent it. The old names still
# resolve, in a fixed order, so nothing breaks mid-rollout.
SPARX_APP_URL=https://app.sparx.works
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=https://app.sparx.works

# Piggles' equivalents. `appOrigin(brand)` derives the variable name from the
# tenant's `platform_brand`, so a second brand is configuration rather than a
# code change — and WITHOUT these, every link the platform builds for a Piggles
# tenant pointed at app.sparx.works. In production an unconfigured origin now
# THROWS rather than guessing, so a missing line here fails the rollout instead
# of quietly mailing somebody another company's URL.
#
# Two, not one, because "where you work" and "where you sign in" are different
# hosts for this brand: mypiggles.com has no sign-in page and never will, so
# anything an unauthenticated person clicks — a team invitation above all — has
# to resolve against the ACCOUNT origin. sparx needs no _ACCOUNT_URL: its
# workbench mounts Better Auth, so the two are the same host and the lookup
# falls through.
PIGGLES_APP_URL=https://mypiggles.com
PIGGLES_ACCOUNT_URL=https://getpiggles.com

# Who the platform speaks AS, per brand (@wizeworks/brand-core). Shared code — an
# email worker draining one queue for both brands — cannot import either brand's
# package, so the identity arrives as configuration keyed by
# `tenants.platform_brand`.
#
# `*_BRAND_NAME` is the case-sensitive product name and is not derivable: sparx
# is lowercase on purpose, Piggles is capitalised on purpose. It is the wordmark
# on every platform email to a tenant with no branding of its own — which is
# most of them early on, and all of which said "sparx" until 2026-08-16.
SPARX_BRAND_NAME=sparx
PIGGLES_BRAND_NAME=Piggles

# PIGGLES_SUPPORT_EMAIL is DELETED, and the deletion is the answer rather than an
# omission. It was set to support@meetpiggles.com pending confirmation; checked
# on 2026-08-17, meetpiggles.com and getpiggles.com have NO MX RECORDS (only
# mypiggles.com does, on smtp.google.com). That address cannot receive mail, so
# as a reply-to it bounces every Piggles support reply — invisibly to the person
# who sent it, who believes they have replied and waits.
#
# Unset means "this brand has published no address": the reply-to and the billing
# line are both omitted, which says nothing rather than something false. To turn
# it back on, put MX on a Piggles domain or use an address on one that already
# has it, then set the variable — no code change.
# The brand's public home — the legal line at the foot of every platform email
# ("WizeWorks · sparx.works"), which was a literal and therefore said sparx on a
# Piggles invoice regardless of how branded that tenant was.
SPARX_BRAND_URL=https://sparx.works
PIGGLES_BRAND_URL=https://meetpiggles.com

# How many TRAILING characters of the name wear the accent color where it is set
# as a wordmark. sparx is "spar" + an Ember "x", so 1; a brand that says nothing
# gets its name set plainly, which is the right default for any third one.
SPARX_BRAND_ACCENT_CHARS=1

# The free-subdomain zone a NEW tenant of this brand is minted in. Distinct from
# SPARX_ZONE_DOMAINS, which is the list of zones this DEPLOYMENT serves — this is
# the one a given brand's signup picks out of it. Without it, a Piggles tenant
# created by any path that did not pass a zone explicitly (a Google signup, an
# invited owner) got its site on sparx.zone.
PIGGLES_ZONE_DOMAIN=piggles.site

# The billing PLAN a Piggles tenant is born on (@wizeworks/billing/plans). Stamped
# once onto `tenants.billing_plan` at provisioning; billing reads the column and
# never asks about the brand again.
#
# It MUST be set wherever Piggles tenants are created. The fallback is `modules` —
# sparx's per-module subscription, on sparx's Stripe account — and a Piggles business
# billed that way looks completely normal until somebody reads the invoice.
PIGGLES_BILLING_PLAN=piggles

# The starter SITE a Piggles business is dressed with at sign-up — a blueprint key
# in the marketplace catalog. `piggles-starter` ships the same six demo goods as the
# golden template under its own invented shop ("Rowan Enamel Mug").
#
# It MUST be set wherever Piggles tenants are created. The fallback is `sparx`, and a
# Piggles salon dressed with that one used to publish a homepage selling literally
# sparx-branded mugs to its clients (issue #091). It no longer would — the golden's
# demo goods and demo business carry no platform name at all now — so what is left at
# stake here is the wrong brand's LOOK rather than the wrong brand's name. Quieter,
# and therefore easier to leave broken.
PIGGLES_GOLDEN_BLUEPRINT=piggles-starter

# The billing PLANS this deployment knows (@wizeworks/billing/plans) — how a
# tenant's bill is shaped, and which Stripe ACCOUNT it is raised in.
#
# It belongs in THIS ConfigMap and not in piggles-app-env, because the service that
# actually bills is api-rest, and api-rest reads sparx-app-env + sparx-app-secrets
# only. A Piggles-shaped value in a shared map looks wrong and is not: the platform
# ships one built-in plan (`modules`) and takes every other as configuration, which
# is exactly how a brand's pricing stays out of shared code.
#
# GENERATED from piggles/config/billing-plan.json — do not hand-edit. `pnpm
# check:billing-plans` fails the push if the two drift, and `--print` regenerates
# this line. Drift here is silent and expensive: the product quotes one price while
# Stripe charges another, and nothing errors.
#
# The env vars it NAMES (PIGGLES_STRIPE_SECRET_KEY, the price ids, the webhook
# secret) are not here — they are secrets, and live in SPARX_APP_SECRETS_ENV.
BILLING_PLANS='[{"id":"piggles","label":"Flat monthly","shape":"flat","secretEnv":"PIGGLES_STRIPE_SECRET_KEY","webhookSecretEnv":"PIGGLES_STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET_BILLING","base":{"product":"piggles_base","lookupKey":"piggles_base_monthly","priceEnv":"PIGGLES_STRIPE_PRICE_BASE_MONTHLY","monthlyCents":9900},"included":{"businesses":1,"locations":1,"sites":1,"seats":3,"contacts":10000,"storage":25,"email":5000},"capacity":[{"key":"seats","label":"Extra team member","product":"piggles_seat","lookupKey":"piggles_seat_monthly","priceEnv":"PIGGLES_STRIPE_PRICE_SEATS","monthlyCents":500,"blockSize":1},{"key":"locations","label":"Extra location","product":"piggles_location","lookupKey":"piggles_location_monthly","priceEnv":"PIGGLES_STRIPE_PRICE_LOCATIONS","monthlyCents":1900,"blockSize":1},{"key":"sites","label":"Extra site","product":"piggles_site","lookupKey":"piggles_site_monthly","priceEnv":"PIGGLES_STRIPE_PRICE_SITES","monthlyCents":1000,"blockSize":1},{"key":"storage","label":"Storage, 10 GB","product":"piggles_storage_10gb","lookupKey":"piggles_storage_10gb_monthly","priceEnv":"PIGGLES_STRIPE_PRICE_STORAGE","monthlyCents":500,"blockSize":10,"blockUnit":"GB"},{"key":"email","label":"Email sends, 5,000 a month","product":"piggles_email_5k","lookupKey":"piggles_email_5k_monthly","priceEnv":"PIGGLES_STRIPE_PRICE_EMAIL","monthlyCents":1000,"blockSize":5000,"blockUnit":"sends"},{"key":"contacts","label":"Customer records, 10,000","product":"piggles_contacts_10k","lookupKey":"piggles_contacts_10k_monthly","priceEnv":"PIGGLES_STRIPE_PRICE_CONTACTS","monthlyCents":1000,"blockSize":10000,"blockUnit":"records"}]}]'
# sparx's own, stated rather than left to a fallback. The same variable is ALSO
# the legacy singular form of SPARX_ZONE_DOMAINS above — a collision that reads
# oddly and is load-bearing either way, because every reader of the list prefers
# the plural, so setting this changes nothing about which zones are served.
#
# What it DOES do is make sparx's brand identity complete, and the site renderer
# now needs that: it refuses to serve a tenant on a zone belonging to another
# brand, and it works out which zone is whose from these values. Absent, the
# check has nothing to compare a sparx tenant against and silently skips it —
# leaving exactly half the cross-brand hole open.
SPARX_ZONE_DOMAIN=sparx.zone
# The attribution badge on every tenant PUBLIC site — the accent on the brand's
# name, and where the badge links.
#
# A literal hex, not a token: the badge renders on a tenant's own site and must
# be immune to that tenant's CSS, so it inlines every value it paints. A token
# would resolve against the tenant's theme and the badge would change colour from
# one site to the next.
#
# Unset is safe — the name renders in one weight. Wrong is not: this badge is on
# the most public surface the platform has, and until 2026-08-16 every Piggles
# business's footer said "Made with sparx" and linked their visitors to it.
SPARX_BRAND_ACCENT=#e04631
PIGGLES_BRAND_ACCENT=#FF6F86
SPARX_CREDIT_URL=https://sparx.works/?utm_source=powered_by&utm_medium=site_badge&utm_campaign=made_with_sparx
PIGGLES_CREDIT_URL=https://meetpiggles.com/?utm_source=powered_by&utm_medium=site_badge&utm_campaign=made_with_piggles
PIGGLES_BRAND_ACCENT_CHARS=0

# The badge's OTHER treatment: a drawn logo instead of the name set as type.
#
# `accentChars` above renders a NAME — right for sparx, whose mark is its
# letterforms with an Ember "x", and wrong for a logo with a shape in it. The
# Piggles wordmark carries a pink dot over the "i"; set as bold Inter it was the
# word and none of the mark.
#
# It arrives as geometry rather than as an import because the renderer,
# wizeworks/apps/site, serves the public sites of BOTH brands from one
# deployment and may not import either brand's package (check-boundaries RULE 1).
#
# DO NOT HAND-EDIT. The drawing in piggles/packages/brand/src/marks.ts is the
# source; this is its serialisation, written by
# `pnpm check:brand-wordmark --update`, and the check fails the build if the two
# ever disagree. A silently stale copy here renders a perfectly clean version of
# last year's logo on every tenant site the platform serves.
PIGGLES_BRAND_WORDMARK='{"viewBox":"0 0 531.08 188.86","paths":["M47.36,127.6c-.16,7.64-7.67,11.53-13.58,11.32-5.84-.21-12.91-4.44-12.89-11.65l.15-62.59c.06-23.04,19.94-39.21,42-39.27,25.42-.07,44.25,19.46,43.21,44.7-.73,17.89-11.94,32.39-29.11,37.36-9.64,2.79-19.27,2.69-29.32-.94l-.45,21.07ZM59.08,51.67c-10.1,2.8-13.77,12.71-11.2,20.95,2.69,8.66,11.6,12.9,19.97,10.85,9.02-2.21,13.87-11.43,11.29-20.52-2.24-7.89-10.54-13.92-20.06-11.28","M140.95,124.07c.03,8.52-4.79,13.62-11.71,14.55-6.6.88-15.05-3.22-15.05-11.4l.02-55.34c0-7.66,7.39-12.07,13.88-11.77,6.28.29,12.64,5.37,12.67,12.96l.19,51.01Z","M166.03,163.85c-6.52-3.63-7.77-10.57-5.16-15.82,5.79-11.65,18.39-1.59,27.91-1.43,8.06.14,14.63-4.59,15.41-12.77-12.67,6.9-25.98,5.8-38.2-1.29-10.18-5.91-16.92-17.94-17.67-31.12-1.43-24.96,17.2-43.46,42.2-42.85,21.17.52,39.85,15.49,39.88,37.5l.04,34.2c.01,12.1-5.53,24.44-15.35,31.51-14.2,10.22-33.77,10.59-49.06,2.06M187.59,83.12c-9.21,1.14-14.17,9.81-13.14,17.44,1.14,8.42,8.51,13.65,16.5,12.85,8.57-.86,14.25-8,13.47-16.7-.66-7.43-7.6-14.74-16.83-13.59","M254.85,163.87c-6.33-3.57-7.68-10.69-5.01-15.75,6.72-12.75,19.86.46,31.65-1.83,6.53-1.27,11.12-5.67,11.45-12.3-12.5,6.74-26.06,5.7-38.09-1.45-10.25-6.1-16.87-18.33-17.44-31.52-1.07-24.51,17.37-43.05,41.98-42.43,21.26.53,40,15.45,39.97,37.58l-.04,35.43c-.01,12.25-5.97,24.09-16.02,30.85-14.3,9.61-33.31,9.96-48.44,1.44M276.48,83.24c-9.28,1.17-14.07,9.58-13.06,17.18,1.15,8.63,8.41,13.67,16.48,12.94,8.58-.79,14.09-8.15,13.39-16.67-.63-7.62-7.69-14.6-16.81-13.44","M355.08,126.81c.02,8.27-7.46,11.94-13.6,11.99-5.47.04-13.21-4.18-13.21-11.42l.04-91.48c0-7.06,7.93-10.33,13.23-10.41,5.05-.07,13.32,3.37,13.34,10.49l.2,90.82Z","M389.21,108.26c7.32,11.66,19.39,9.07,29.02,5.16,5.89-2.39,12.49-.11,14.99,4.57,2.87,5.39,1.18,12.31-4.7,15.37-15.47,8.05-34.97,8.99-49.78-1.49-20.74-14.68-22.22-45.95-3.71-63.06,11.62-10.74,28.24-12.62,42.62-7.46,13.38,4.81,22.24,16.38,23.35,30.55.78,9.32-6.19,16.17-15.52,16.21l-36.26.15ZM416.3,91.09c-2.26-9.06-8.39-10.99-14.46-10.8-5.84.18-11.41,3.71-13.56,10.94l28.02-.14Z","M495.9,135.77c-14.33,6.22-30.65,4.35-43.21-3.72-5.46-3.51-6.17-10.7-3.77-14.96,3.1-5.51,10.15-6.86,15.91-3.93,10.39,5.28,19.58,6.16,20.21,1.31.15-1.12-1.88-3.55-3.13-3.94l-17.35-5.43c-9.28-2.9-15.57-9.98-16.39-18.66-.84-8.81,2.71-17.86,11.01-22.67,13.02-7.54,29.99-7.17,42.58,1.03,4.67,3.04,5.65,9.7,4.06,13.56-2.29,5.59-8.38,7.53-14.51,5.06-8.75-3.52-17.43-5.36-18.19-.32-.16,1.06,1.68,3.62,2.76,3.92l16.8,4.72c10.17,2.86,16.68,10.92,17.47,20.32.83,9.87-4.05,19.28-14.26,23.71"],"accentPath":"M120.67,27.21c8.18-4.16,16.91-.79,20.25,6.61,3.14,6.97.85,15.85-6.19,19.66-6.91,3.74-15.62,1.18-19.46-4.96-4.36-6.97-2.61-17.24,5.41-21.32"}'

# --- The email palette -----------------------------------------------------
# The colors a PLATFORM email paints itself in — masthead band, the one action,
# receipt rules, footer well. One JSON object per brand, because a palette is ONE
# decision and half a palette (this brand's accent on that brand's ink) is the
# only outcome worse than none. Hex rather than tokens because mail clients strip
# <style>; six digits only, since 3- and 8-digit forms do not survive Outlook.
#
# UNSET IS SAFE AND VISIBLE: the brand sends in an achromatic fallback that
# belongs to nobody, and the worker warns naming this variable. Deliberately NOT
# sparx's values as the default — that is how the Ember masthead reached every
# Piggles owner. Rationale + the collapse rules for the optional roles:
# wizeworks/packages/brand-core/src/email-palette.ts.
SPARX_EMAIL_PALETTE={"accent":"#e04631","accentContent":"#ffffff","accentEdge":"#c13a28","accentWash":"#fbe9e5","ink":"#0c1433","inkContent":"#ffffff","inkMeta":"#97a0bd","paper":"#ffffff","canvas":"#eceef2","well":"#fafafc","line":"#e8eaf0","lineStrong":"#d3d7e0","heading":"#0c1433","body":"#2b3242","lead":"#3a4152","label":"#4b5563","meta":"#5b6472","success":"#0f8a5f","successWash":"#e7f6ef","warnInk":"#7c3a06","warnWash":"#fbeed9","danger":"#c13a28","dangerWash":"#fdece9","info":"#3b5bdb","infoWash":"#eef2ff","dark":{"background":"#0b1120","foreground":"#e2e8f0","muted":"#111827","border":"#1f2937"}}
#
# Piggles carries only what its approved board states, mapped by ROLE: primary ->
# accent, secondary -> ink, base-100/200/300 -> paper/well/canvas+line,
# base-content -> heading+body, neutral -> meta. accentEdge, accentWash, inkMeta
# and lineStrong are ABSENT because the board has no value for them and computing
# one from the primary would be inventing a Piggles color — they collapse (flat
# button, hairline step chip, full-strength masthead meta, ordinary underline)
# until Brandon supplies four values.
PIGGLES_EMAIL_PALETTE={"accent":"#ff6f86","accentContent":"#ffffff","ink":"#2d3443","inkContent":"#ffffff","paper":"#ffffff","canvas":"#eef0f3","well":"#f7f8fa","line":"#eef0f3","heading":"#202631","body":"#202631","meta":"#52454f","dark":{"background":"#272d39","foreground":"#f4f5f7","muted":"#1c212c","border":"#151922","primary":"#ff7c91","primaryForeground":"#20151a"}}

# --- Capacity allowances ---------------------------------------------------
# What each brand INCLUDES, per tenant. Metering is platform (every tenant is
# measured nightly, see @wizeworks/usage); the ceilings are commercial and
# therefore per brand — sparx charges per active module and caps almost nothing,
# Piggles charges one flat rate and capacity IS its product.
#
# A meter that is absent here is METERED WITHOUT A CEILING: usage is shown,
# because it is measured, and no bar, percentage or warning is drawn, because
# there is nothing true to draw them against. That is the honest rendering of a
# number nobody has decided, and it is what lets metering ship ahead of pricing —
# which it must, since usage history cannot be backfilled.
#
# SPARX_CAPACITY is deliberately UNSET. sparx sells per module, not per
# capacity; every meter reads unmetered and every sparx tenant sees consumption
# with no ceiling, which is exactly right.
#
# Piggles carries only the four allowances its pricing sheet states outright.
# storageBytes and emailSendsPerMonth are ABSENT because that sheet gives them as
# RANGES it has not settled — "10–25 GB", "5,000–10,000 sends/month", under
# "Final numbers must be validated against infrastructure cost." Picking the
# middle of an undecided range and rendering it as a limit manufactures a
# commercial decision out of a placeholder, and the owner reading "12.4 GB of 25
# GB" cannot tell it from a real one. Set them when the two numbers are decided;
# nothing in the code changes.
PIGGLES_CAPACITY={"seats":3,"sites":1,"locations":1,"contacts":10000}



NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://api.sparx.works
NEXT_PUBLIC_MARKETING_URL=https://sparx.works
SPARX_PUBLIC_API_REST_URL=https://api.sparx.works

# --- The zones we own ------------------------------------------------------
# Comma-separated, and the FIRST entry is the default — the zone anything that
# does not name one is minted in. This is what lets ONE api-rest serve two
# brands' tenant sites: a sparx business gets <slug>.sparx.zone, a Piggles
# business gets <slug>.piggles.site (piggles/CLAUDE.md, "The three surfaces").
#
# Read by services/api-rest/src/lib/domain.ts, and every consequence flows from
# it: whether a host counts as ours, whether Caddy's on-demand TLS may mint a
# certificate for it, the bare-<tenant>.<zone> routing fallback, and which CNAME
# target a customer is told to point their own domain at. Drop piggles.site and
# every Piggles tenant site stops resolving.
#
# The singular SPARX_ZONE_DOMAIN still works and means a one-entry list.
SPARX_ZONE_DOMAINS=sparx.zone,piggles.site

# --- In-cluster addresses --------------------------------------------------
# Namespace is still sparx-prod (see kustomization.yaml for why), so these are
# byte-identical to production.
SPARX_API_REST_URL=http://api-rest.sparx-prod.svc.cluster.local:3000
SPARX_DASHBOARD_INTERNAL_URL=http://workbench.sparx-prod.svc.cluster.local:3000
# REDIS_URL is deliberately ABSENT. It exists only to fan socket.io chat messages
# between api-rest replicas; at replicas:1 the in-memory adapter is correct, and
# the app treats "unset" as exactly that. Set it the day api-rest scales to 2.

# --- Media -----------------------------------------------------------------
# GCS_MEDIA_BUCKET / GCS_MEDIA_PUBLIC_BUCKET are ABSENT, which is what selects
# the local-disk backend in packages/media/src/storage.ts (`getStorage` falls
# through to LocalStorage when the bucket env is unset). MEDIA_LOCAL_DIR is
# backed by a PVC shared by api-rest and media-worker — the worker writes
# variants, api-rest streams them, so they MUST see the same volume.
MEDIA_LOCAL_DIR=/media
MEDIA_PUBLIC_URL=https://media.sparx.works

# --- Email -----------------------------------------------------------------
# `mailgun` SENDS; `console` renders the message and logs it instead.
#
# This was `console` in production on BOTH clouds — the value here was inherited
# from the GCP configmap, and the live GKE ConfigMap read `console` too. So every
# message routed through @wizeworks/email's provider (packages/email/src/providers/
# index.ts — password resets, team invitations, order mail, the OTP escape hatch)
# was rendered and logged rather than sent, by email-worker that consumed its
# events and acked them looking perfectly healthy. Mail HAS been observed
# arriving from the platform, so some path evidently bypasses this selector;
# that path has not been identified and is not this one.
#
# BOTH VARS ARE REQUIRED. getEmailProvider() THROWS when `mailgun` is selected
# without SPARX_MAILGUN_DOMAIN, and email-worker resolves the provider at boot
# (services/email-worker/src/index.ts) — so setting the provider alone does not
# degrade to console, it CrashLoops the worker. The domain was never configured
# in any overlay, which is why it is new here rather than copied across.
#
# SPARX_POSTAL_URL is dropped: Postal is decommissioned and its Service does not
# exist in this overlay.
SPARX_EMAIL_PROVIDER=mailgun
SPARX_MAILGUN_DOMAIN=sparx.email

# --- Search ----------------------------------------------------------------
TYPESENSE_HOST=typesense
TYPESENSE_PORT=8108
TYPESENSE_PROTOCOL=http
# commerce-indexer creates any MISSING Typesense collection at boot. Unset it
# defaults to false, and this cluster's Typesense came up on an empty PVC — the
# search index is the one piece of state that lives on a disk rather than in
# Postgres, so it did not come across with the database and there were no
# collections at all. Every index write then returned
#   Request failed with HTTP code 404 | Server said: Not Found
# and a full reindex of all 11 tenants nak-ed and redelivered forever while the
# API cheerfully returned 200 to each request. Search was empty platform-wide.
#
# ON is the correct steady state, not a first-deploy toggle. ensureSchemas()
# retrieves each collection and only CREATES on a 404; on an existing one it adds
# missing OPTIONAL fields and nothing else. It never drops or recreates — that is
# dropAllSchemas(), a separate function nothing here calls. So the documented
# fear ("a misconfigured deploy might recreate an indexed collection") is not a
# thing this code path can do, while the cost of leaving it off is a silently
# empty index on every fresh cluster.
ENSURE_SCHEMAS_ON_BOOT=true

